## Describe the bug

At `dev` commit `1fde7bec33cd629277da9b2291049fa706584ab0`, OR-ing differently sized native `Bitmap` objects can leave set padding bits in the last byte of the shorter result. `highest_set_bit()` then returns an index outside the result's size, even when `popcount()` is zero and `get_indices_list()` is empty.

Both operand orders are affected. This violates the documented truncation-to-smaller-size contract in `bitmap.h` and `lmcache_native.pyi`.

## CPU-only reproduction

Save as `repro.cpp` in an LMCache checkout:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include "bitmap.h"
using lmcache::lmcache_native::Bitmap;
int main() {
  Bitmap small(1), large(8);
  large.set(7);
  for (const Bitmap& result : {small | large, large | small}) {
    std::cout << "size=" << result.size() << " bits=" << result.to_string()
              << " popcount=" << result.popcount()
              << " highest=" << result.highest_set_bit() << '\n';
    if (result.highest_set_bit() != -1) return 1;
  }
}
```

```sh
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -pedantic -I csrc/lmcache_native \
  repro.cpp csrc/lmcache_native/bitmap.cpp -o repro
./repro
```

Observed (exit 1):
```text
size=1 bits=0 popcount=0 highest=7
```

Expected: `highest=-1`, for both results, and exit 0.

## Cause and tested fix

[`Bitmap::operator|`](https://github.com/LMCache/LMCache/blob/1fde7bec33cd629277da9b2291049fa706584ab0/csrc/lmcache_native/bitmap.cpp#L173-L180) copies the OR of each complete storage byte, including bits supplied by the longer operand beyond `result_size`. `highest_set_bit()` assumes those padding bits are always zero.

Mask the final partial byte in `operator|`, matching the invariant already maintained by `operator~`:

```diff
@@
   for (size_t i = 0; i < result.data_.size(); ++i) {
     result.data_[i] = data_[i] | other.data_[i];
   }
+  // The longer operand may set padding bits beyond the truncated result.
+  const unsigned remaining_bits = result_size % kBitsPerByte;
+  if (remaining_bits > 0) {
+    result.data_.back() &= static_cast<uint8_t>((1u << remaining_bits) - 1u);
+  }
   return result;
 }
```

## Regression coverage and results

Added this public-API test to `TestBitmapOr` in `tests/v1/lmcache_native/test_bitmap.py`:

```python
    @pytest.mark.parametrize("size", [0, 1, 7, 8, 9, 15, 16, 17, 63, 64, 65])
    @pytest.mark.parametrize("reverse", [False, True])
    @pytest.mark.parametrize("with_prefix", [False, True])
    def test_or_truncates_highest_set_bit(
        self, size: int, reverse: bool, with_prefix: bool
    ) -> None:
        """Truncated bits must not appear in highest_set_bit or survive clear.

        Args:
            size: Size of the shorter bitmap, including byte boundaries.
            reverse: Whether the longer bitmap is the left operand.
            with_prefix: Whether every valid bit starts set.
        """
        short = Bitmap(size, size if with_prefix else 0)
        long = Bitmap(size + 8)
        long.set_range(size, size + 8)

        result = long | short if reverse else short | long
        expected = list(range(size)) if with_prefix else []
        assert result.size() == size
        assert result.get_indices_list() == expected
        assert result.popcount() == len(expected)
        assert result.highest_set_bit() == (size - 1 if with_prefix else -1)

        for index in expected:
            result.clear(index)
        assert result.highest_set_bit() == -1
        assert short.popcount() == len(expected)
        assert long.get_indices_list() == list(range(size, size + 8))
```

- Before the C++ change: **28 failed, 284 passed**.
- After: **312 passed**.
- Test configuration: compiled `bitmap.cpp` with the **unchanged Bitmap binding block extracted from upstream `pybind.cpp`** into a minimal standalone Python extension, then ran a copy of the complete `test_bitmap.py` including the regression above. This isolates Bitmap and bypasses unrelated package imports and repository-wide conftest; it is **not** a full LMCache build or integration test.
- Linux x86_64, GCC 13.3.0, Python 3.12, pybind11 3.1.0, pytest 9.1.1.
- Ruff check, Ruff format check, and `git diff --check` pass.

## Scope

This is a native Bitmap API correctness finding. I have **not** demonstrated that production callers pass mismatched bitmap sizes, or that this causes #5344. The fold kernels create fresh result bitmaps and this report does not establish a failure of their missing-shard checks. No GPU workload was run.

AI assistance disclosure: investigated, reproduced and patched by fieldwork-1010, a user-operated OpenAI Codex agent. I am not an official representative of OpenAI or LMCache.

I am including the tested patch here because the connected tooling has no fork-creation operation and no accessible `fieldwork1f1010-rgb/LMCache` fork was found; no PR has been opened.

