## Summary

`server_prompt_cache`, the RAM-backed cross-slot prompt/KV cache behind `--cache-ram` (default: enabled, 8192 MiB) and `--cache-idle-slots` (default: enabled), can restore content from a **completely unrelated, already-finished conversation** into a slot serving a **new, unrelated request**, and the model then generates a continuation of that stale conversation instead of answering the actual prompt. This reproduces with `--kv-unified` explicitly off (`-np 2`, no `-kvu`), so it is a distinct mechanism from #25992.

The API's own `usage.prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens` accounting shows `0` in every corrupted response observed, there is no client-visible signal that this happened.

## Root cause

`--cache-ram` (default 8192 MiB) and `--cache-idle-slots` (default `true`) are both **enabled by default** and not opt-in:

https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/adb55e5148dc93bcdca7212a2d1df3ccc422959a/common/common.h#L612-L615


With these on, `cache_idle_slots` publishes a snapshot of any idle slot's current prompt into the shared, cross-slot `server_prompt_cache` pool on **every task launch** (not just at eviction):

https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/adb55e5148dc93bcdca7212a2d1df3ccc422959a/tools/server/server-context.cpp#L2352-L2367


Note the idle slot's own live VRAM KV/`prompt.tokens` is **not** cleared when `kv_unified` is false, only a RAM copy is published. So with `-np 2` (our config; `kv_unified` defaults false since `-np` is never `auto`), the pool is refreshed on nearly every request, and each slot keeps its own stale prompt history live until something overwrites it.

When `get_available_slot()` can't find a live slot with ≥`slot_prompt_similarity` (default 0.1) LCP against a new task, it falls back to LRU-evicting a slot, then tries the pool:

https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/adb55e5148dc93bcdca7212a2d1df3ccc422959a/tools/server/server-context.cpp#L1577-L1594

```cpp
if (ret) {
    update_cache = update_cache && prompt_cache;
    update_cache = update_cache && task.type == SERVER_TASK_TYPE_COMPLETION;

    if (update_cache) {
        ret->prompt_save(*prompt_cache);

        if (!ret->prompt_load(*prompt_cache, task.tokens)) {
            ret->prompt_clear();
        }
        ...
```

`prompt_load()`'s return value gates `prompt_clear()`, but the return value doesn't mean what the caller assumes:

https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/adb55e5148dc93bcdca7212a2d1df3ccc422959a/tools/server/server-task.cpp#L1790-L1815

```cpp
bool server_prompt_cache::load(server_prompt & prompt, const server_tokens & tokens_new, llama_context * ctx_tgt, llama_context * ctx_dft, int32_t id_slot) {
    ...
    auto it_best = states.end();

    for (auto it = states.begin(); it != states.end(); ++it) {
        const int lcp_cur = it->prompt.tokens.get_common_prefix(tokens_new);
        const float f_keep_cur = float(lcp_cur) / it->prompt.tokens.size();
        const float f_sim_cur  = float(lcp_cur) / tokens_new.size();

        // don't trash large prompts
        if (f_keep_cur < 0.25f) {
            continue;
        }
        ...
    }

    if (it_best != states.end()) {
        // restore full state via llama_state_seq_set_data_ext(), reassign prompt = it_best->prompt
    }

    return true; // <-- returns true (no-op) when it_best == states.end(), i.e. no match found
}
```

`load()` returns `true` even when `it_best == states.end()` (no match found), so `prompt_clear()` **never fires** in the normal "nothing in the pool is similar enough" case. The slot's `prompt.tokens` and live VRAM KV are left exactly as they were pre-eviction: a full, unrelated, already-finished conversation. Only `prompt_save()` ran, non-destructively snapshotting a copy elsewhere.

The main decode path later recomputes a real LCP against the actual new prompt and truncates (`keep_first(n_past)` + `mem.seq_rm(slot.id, p0, -1)`) before decoding, this is presumably intended to fully purge the stale content regardless of the above. Empirically, under concurrent two-slot load, it does not reliably do so (see repro below); we have not fully traced why that truncation is insufficient, only that it demonstrably is.

## Repro

Hardware: 2x AMD Strix Halo (gfx1151, ROCm/HIP), `llama-server` built at merge commit `a0e27a3e4` (`origin/master` = `adb55e514` above, merged onto our fork with two local, non-`tools/server` patches, confirmed byte-identical to upstream for every file quoted here).

```
--model <Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q8_0.gguf> --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8080 --n-gpu-layers 99 \
--ctx-size 393216 --parallel 2 --cache-type-k q8_0 --cache-type-v q8_0 --flash-attn on \
--threads 16 --ubatch-size 2048 --direct-io --jinja \
--chat-template-file Qwen3.5-4B.jinja --metrics --timeout 240
```

(No `--cache-ram`, `--cache-idle-slots`, or `--kv-unified` passed, all three defaults apply. `-np 2` means `kv_unified` is genuinely `false` per `server.cpp`'s `n_parallel < 0` check, ruling out #25992's mechanism directly.)

Script (pseudocode, full version available on request):
1. Send several sequential `/v1/chat/completions` requests, each with a unique, randomly-generated ~1200-word system prompt + a user message + an assistant `tool_calls` turn + a `tool` result turn (~4500 tokens total), each containing a unique marker string and instructing the model to echo a derived "secret code" back. This seeds the RAM pool with several distinct, finished conversations.
2. Fire pairs of further such requests **concurrently** (two threads, same instant), each with its own fresh unique marker.
3. Check whether each response's content contains its own request's marker/code, or a different one.

Result across two batches (20 rounds / 40 requests, then 8 rounds / 16 requests, after seeding): **20/20 and 8/8 rounds produced at least one corrupted response**, content belonging to a marker from an earlier, unrelated, already-completed request (in the worst run, the *same* stale marker string repeated verbatim across 19 con

# SM70 V100 Volta - Crashes using CUDA Flash Attention for Non-Standard Dimension Heads

## Overview

- Error - CUDA error: invalid argument current device: 0, in function ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_mma_f16_case at llama.cpp/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/template-instances/../fattn-mma-f16.cuh:1945

- Issue: Some newer models such as Mistral-4 do not use standard dimension head sizes. This causes SM70 architecture to crash when using CUDA Flash Attention.

- Fix: Check dimension head for non-standard size and fallback to cuBLAS layer
*(Previously submitted coded solution, only effects V100 Volta SM70)*:
File Affected In proposed Patch: ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn.cu
```cu
    // (Check for SM70 V100 Volta arch)
    // Corrects crashes with CUDA Flash-Attention in some newer model architecture
    // Example: Mistral-Small-4
    if (cc == GGML_CUDA_CC_VOLTA) {
        int dkq = Q->ne[0];

        // If non-standard (power-of-two head size: 64, 128, 256)
        // Use cuBLAS fallback layer.
        if (dkq != 64 && dkq != 128 && dkq != 256) {
            return BEST_FATTN_KERNEL_NONE;
        }
    }
```

## Error Repoduction Steps
- Clone/Download llama.cpp source code from repository
- Configure and compile llama.cpp source code using CUDA Flash-Attention = ON
- Attempt to start inferencing with newer type model architectures (eg: Mistral-4)
- Result: Crash of llama.cpp pointing to **llama.cpp/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/template-instances/../fattn-mma-f16.cuh**




## Reason for creating issue:

- I seemed to have created a pull request with the code included to resolve the issue which would only affect a V100 Volta and not any other outlying arch types- in error. According to @IMbacKK who was nice enough to advise me in the appropriate steps for which I should take, message is quoted below.

not the right fix, file an issue with reproduction steps instead.

_Originally posted by @IMbackK in https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/issues/28640#issuecomment-5599781682_

### Name and Version

llama.cpp upstream master `8b543610254aafe121942eaf79a23c3847177270`, tested on October 9, 2026.
Apple M4 Pro, 64 GB unified memory, macOS 26.6.2.

### Operating systems

Mac

### Which llama.cpp modules do you know to be affected?

Other: ggml Metal backend, `ggml/src/ggml-metal/kernels/mul_mv.metal`.

### Problem description & steps to reproduce

This follows up on point 4 of the [review of PR #30072](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/pull/30072#issuecomment-6043629116).

The destination offset in [`mul_vec_q_n_f32_impl`](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/8b543610254aafe121942eaf79a23c3847177270/ggml/src/ggml-metal/kernels/mul_mv.metal#L293) is calculated with 32-bit integers:

```cpp
device float * dst_f32 = (device float *) dst + im*args.ne0*args.ne1 + r1*args.ne0;
```

I reproduced an overflow with Q4_0 src0 `[128, 1048576]` and F32 src1 `[128, 1, 2050]`, broadcasting src0 across the batches. The F32 output contains 2,149,580,800 elements. The operation returns success, but batches 2048 and 2049 are never written: 2,097,152 elements remain at the NaN sentinel value. Their batch offsets exceed `INT32_MAX`.

Casting the two offset products to `uint64_t` fixes this reproducer. After the change, every output element passes comparison with a host reference. The checker uses dequantized Q4_0 values for the reference and a tolerance of `0.02 + 0.01*abs(reference)`; observed maximum absolute error is `3.34e-6`.

The helper is used by the plain Q4_0, Q4_1, Q5_0 and Q5_1 matrix-vector kernels. Q4_0 is the type tested here. The corresponding `mul_mv_id` wrappers already calculate the batch offset in 64 bits before calling this helper.

This is a synthetic reproducer; I have not identified a real model that reaches this shape. Each run used about 9.4 GB peak memory. This is separate from the `mul_mm` changes in #30072.

### Command line

Save the two checker source files below as `bigcheck.cpp` and `common.h` in a checkout of the commit above. From the checkout root:

```sh
