### Problem

With local hot caching enabled, `Client::Get` and both `BatchGet` paths promote completed READ data before validating the query's read lease. They can return `LEASE_EXPIRED` while leaving that rejected payload in the hot cache. A later read with a live lease and a same-size replica can return success using those cached bytes.

Audited main: `dcddb56c0cddc96f41eeb8dd82e5bd9f497183a2`.

### Mechanism

- [Single Get](https://github.com/kvcache-ai/Mooncake/blob/dcddb56c0cddc96f41eeb8dd82e5bd9f497183a2/mooncake-store/src/client_service.cpp#L1423-L1446): checksum validation, then admission/`ProcessSlicesAsync`, then `IsLeaseExpired`.
- [Preferred same-node BatchGet](https://github.com/kvcache-ai/Mooncake/blob/dcddb56c0cddc96f41eeb8dd82e5bd9f497183a2/mooncake-store/src/client_service.cpp#L1637-L1662): promotion happens inside the segment completion loop, before the final batch lease check.
- [Regular BatchGet](https://github.com/kvcache-ai/Mooncake/blob/dcddb56c0cddc96f41eeb8dd82e5bd9f497183a2/mooncake-store/src/client_service.cpp#L1882-L1914): the same ordering.
- [SubmitPutTask](https://github.com/kvcache-ai/Mooncake/blob/dcddb56c0cddc96f41eeb8dd82e5bd9f497183a2/mooncake-store/src/local_hot_cache.cpp#L518-L561) synchronously copies the payload, then queues publication with a cache-generation token. That token protects against local invalidation during a fill; it does not validate the query lease.
- [RedirectToHotCache](https://github.com/kvcache-ai/Mooncake/blob/dcddb56c0cddc96f41eeb8dd82e5bd9f497183a2/mooncake-store/src/client_service.cpp#L1932-L1960) looks up the key and size. Thus the rejected payload can affect a subsequent successful read, not just the already-failed request.

### Reproduction and observed results

I added the test below to the existing `client_local_hot_cache_test.cpp` fixture. It uses the real Client/cache paths, registered memory, an in-process master, TCP for the preferred batch path, and SHM hot caching (8 KiB, 4 KiB blocks, admission threshold 1). Single Get and regular BatchGet use the local memcpy path.

For each of single Get, regular BatchGet, and preferred same-node BatchGet:

1. Read 64 `B` bytes using an already-expired `QueryResult`.
2. Confirm the read returns `LEASE_EXPIRED`.
3. Observe admission count **1**, and wait for the key to be published in the cache.
4. Change the registered source buffer in place to 64 `A` bytes and provide a fresh, live `QueryResult`.
5. The fresh Get returns **success but 64 `B` bytes** from the rejected read.

All three modes exhibited both failures in the test: nonzero admission count and wrong payload on the subsequent read. There were no transfer failures in the final registered-buffer reproduction.

This deliberately controls the metadata and source buffer; it does not simulate a master-driven eviction or establish the frequency of this failure in production. It proves that an expired read can publish a cache entry and that entry is subsequently usable. Region reuse after lease expiry is the concerning production case.

**Build note:** current main needed the existing unrelated build prerequisite from #4513 (Environ API removal). I applied those changes only in the validation copy. Neither the Client read paths nor hot-cache implementation was modified.

